UPDATE "gaps" SET "description" = 'scripts/db.py''s _require_verbatim digit-check (add-extraction''s claimed_value verification, ~line 6579) was widened 2026- to accept CJK numerals alongside Arabic digits, but has no path for LATIN-SCRIPT SPELLED-OUT CARDINAL NUMBERS -- e.g. Finnish ''viisi prosenttia''/''kahdeksan prosenttia'' (five/eight percent), which is how Finland''s Decree 241/2017 states its ramp-gradient figures throughout (no digit form appears anywhere in the statute text). A claimed_value written in digit form (''5%'') is refused because no digit character occurs in the verified claim_text; --verbatim-exempt cannot rescue it either, because exemption is refused whenever the claim_text DOES verify against a persisted artefact (it does here). Net effect: a genuinely verbatim-verified numerical claim from a language that spells cardinals as words has NO path to carry a digit-form claimed_value at all. Worked around in batch 22 (REF-01017, Finland, extraction on parameter 3) by setting claimed_value to the source''s own spelled-out form (''viisi prosenttia'') and giving the digit-equivalent only as an editorial gloss in --claimed-unit/--notes, explicitly marked as non-verbatim. Fix belongs in a tooling-only PR (rule 10): extend the digit-check with a small per-language cardinal-number lexicon (or a number-parsing library) analogous to the existing _CJK_DIGITS/_CJK_UNITS tables, or provide an explicit escape that documents a spelled-out-to-digit mapping without requiring the exemption path. R5 (non-English work is academic, not lesser) applies here exactly as it did for the CJK fix this function''s own comment already describes. || CORRECTED 2026-11-06: GAP-090 is the same class for Korean line breaks: a quote cannot cross a break inside a word. || CORRECTED 2026-11-06: CORRECTION to the note appended 2026-11-06 about GAP-090: GAP-090''s premise is wrong (the verbatim check strips all whitespace and the full Korean sentences verify), so this gap is not extended by it.', "updated_at" = '2026-11-06 05:29' WHERE "gap_id" = 'GAP-056';
UPDATE "gaps" SET "description" = 'WHAT TERM NAMES THE WIDTH A PASSAGE NEEDS AT A TURN, AND WHICH PARAMETER HOLDS IT? OWNER DECISION. Found in sessions session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch and session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2 (PRs 211 and 212); the adversarial findings are in adversarial_findings, passes 8 and 9. The Anthropometry of Wheeled Mobility report (REF-00468) measures the width needed for a 90-degree turn, a 180-degree turn in a dead-end passage, and a 180-degree turn around an obstacle, and separately the diameter for a 360-degree turn. TERM-003 (turning circle, parameter 5) is defined as space for 360-degree rotation; TERM-002 (corridor width, parameter 4) is the clear width along a route. A width at a turn is neither. Adjudications 88/89 (observations 97 and 98) DEFERRED it under the owner''s rule that a new term''s definition is the owner''s (OT-2 pattern). The report''s turn-width rows are filed on parameter 4 with the setting stated; the 360-degree rows on parameter 5. CONSEQUENCE until decided: a determination on parameter 5 does not see the 180-degree width, which the report treats as an alternative basis for the minimum turning space and which the ADA says is covered by its 1525 mm turning space. OPTIONS: (a) a new term and parameter for width at a turn; (b) an expression of TERM-002 conditioned on a turn; (c) widen TERM-003. RESOLVE: owner defines the term; then adjudicate observations 97 and 98, and re-point rows by new rows (committed rows are immutable). DERIVE: select extraction_id, parameter_id, claimed_value, claimed_unit, setting from source_value_extractions where ref_id = ''REF-00468'' and parameter_id in (4, 5). || CORRECTED 2026-11-06: RESOLUTION ANALYSIS (Opus, read-only, 2026-11-06; scratchpad/session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2/resolution-analysis-GAP-074-096.md, section for this gap). CONFIRMED. Also the same question as GAP-068 (parameter 5 may name a space, not a quantity): the report states square spaces for the 360-degree turn beside diameters, which no gap lists. Owner question 4 of the sheet. Needs no tooling; unblocks parameter 5.', "updated_at" = '2026-11-06 05:29' WHERE "gap_id" = 'GAP-074';
UPDATE "gaps" SET "description" = 'CAN A CONTROLLED PRIMARY MEASUREMENT REPORT FILED AS grey REACH T1? OWNER DECISION. Found in sessions session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch and session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2 (PRs 211 and 212); the adversarial findings are in adversarial_findings, passes 8 and 9. REF-00468 is a federal contract research report (US Access Board contract, NIDRR grant), not peer reviewed, whose method is incremental mock-wall measurement of several hundred users. governance/tier-system.md line 15 lists T1 as ''clinical, occasionally grey for unpublished primary''. schemas/tier_derivation.py derives (grey, intrinsic) to tier 3 only, so the ladder''s own T1 wording is unreachable, and add-source refuses a tier that disagrees with the derivation (rule 8). The row is filed grey, tier 3, grey_flag 1; changing evidence_type to clinical to reach T1 would misstate what the source is. EFFECT: the largest measured dataset on wheelchair turning and passage widths renders as weak-band (circle) on every row. RESOLVE: owner rules either that the ladder stays (then this gap closes as decided, and the tier-system line is corrected by a DR) or that unpublished controlled primary research derives to T1 (then a tier_derivation change in a tooling PR, then amend-source on REF-00468 with the scope the ladder derives). DERIVE: select ref_id, tier, evidence_type, scope, grey_flag from evidence_sources where ref_id = ''REF-00468''; sed -n 15p governance/tier-system.md; grep -n grey schemas/tier_derivation.py. || CORRECTED 2026-11-06: RESOLUTION ANALYSIS (Opus, read-only, 2026-11-06; scratchpad/session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2/resolution-analysis-GAP-074-096.md, section for this gap). PARTLY. CORRECTION: ''filing it clinical would misstate the source'' is contradicted by governance/evidence-methodology.md line 43, which defines grey as a transitional type for sources not classifiable above (verified by the author), and amend-source --field evidence_type with --tier already exists. REF-01005 and REF-01008 are in the same class. Owner question 3: clinical with the grey flag kept (band filled circle), stay grey tier 3 (band open circle, tier-system line 15 corrected), or a new grey-to-T1 route (tooling).', "updated_at" = '2026-11-06 05:29' WHERE "gap_id" = 'GAP-075';
UPDATE "gaps" SET "description" = 'WHICH LENS DOES A CLAUSE CARRY WHEN ITS OWN TEXT NAMES NO ONE BUT SITS UNDER A DISABILITY HEADING (MOB OR ALL), AND WHO RE-KEYS THE ROWS ALREADY FILED INCONSISTENTLY? OWNER DECISION plus a writer gap. Found in sessions session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch and session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2 (PRs 211 and 212); the adversarial findings are in adversarial_findings, passes 8 and 9. Lens is the reader''s filing at extraction and judgment owns the crossing (CLAUDE.md section 6), but nothing rules the filing convention. Within one code the rows differ: the Korean annex items 6 and 7 are headed as for persons with disabilities and rows 207 to 210 are ALL while row 215 and every prior row of that code are MOB; the ADA door, turning and route rows are MOB while its force row is ALL; the UK Table 2 door rows are ALL while its door-force rows are MOB. Same quantity, two lenses, on nothing in the texts; synthesis keys on the lens. No writer changes an identity_code (amend-extraction takes comparator, extraction_method, extraction_status, figure_role, root_ref_id, root_type only), and a second row duplicates the figure (rule 5 and GAP-048). Adversarial findings 147, 171, 172 are PROVISIONAL-DISPUTED for this. RESOLVE: (1) owner rules the convention (MOB where the clause or its heading names disabled people, else ALL, is the one the later rows used, and is a proposal, not a ruling); (2) a writer to re-key a lens with the replaced value recorded (SW gap), or judgment supersedes by new rows. DERIVE: select ref_id, identity_code, count(*) from source_value_extractions where ref_id in (''REF-00468'',''REF-00987'',''REF-00992'',''REF-01028'',''REF-01031'',''REF-01032'') group by 1, 2. || CORRECTED 2026-11-06: RESOLUTION ANALYSIS (Opus, read-only, 2026-11-06; scratchpad/session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2/resolution-analysis-GAP-074-096.md, section for this gap). PARTLY. CORRECTION: ''nothing rules the convention'' is wrong. Rulings OR-1 and OR-1b fix