UPDATE "gaps" SET "description" = 'A RESULT PAYLOAD CAN BE LINKED TO A SEARCH ONLY IF THE SEARCH''S query_text NAMES THE PAYLOAD''S HOST, AND A WebSearch LEAVES NO PAYLOAD AT ALL. TOOLING PR (rule 10). Found in sessions session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch and session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2 (PRs 211 and 212); the adversarial findings are in adversarial_findings, passes 8 and 9. Europe PMC checks that support ''no open full text'' (persisted as 198b3da3fb3d7b6a.json, c4c1f76df9877588.json) cannot be linked to the PubMed search they support, and the corada pages and the Anthropometry PDF could be linked to the lookups only after the query text was suffixed with the URL (executions 203 and 202 carry or lack that). The result list of a WebSearch exists only in the transcript; the three web searches here were logged with notes written from it and the critic had to rebuild the lists from main.jsonl (the first search''s link count was mis-stated and corrected). This is the WebFetch problem of GAP-034 for the search tool. RESOLVE: let a result artefact link on the artefact''s own manifest purpose or ref_id, and persist a WebSearch result list as a retrieval-log artefact (the hook has it). DERIVE: select exec_id, query_text from search_executions where engine = ''web'' and created_by_session = ''session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2''; grep -c europepmc retrieval-log/session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch/manifest.jsonl. || CORRECTED 2026-11-06: RESOLUTION ANALYSIS (Opus, read-only, 2026-11-06; scratchpad/session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2/resolution-analysis-GAP-074-096.md, section for this gap). PARTLY. The refusal is the ratified DR-2026-09-26 section 2.2(b) design, not a defect; the WebSearch half duplicates GAP-034. Resolve in data: log the Europe PMC lookups as their own executions with log-search. Close as accepted.', "updated_at" = '2026-11-06 05:29' WHERE "gap_id" = 'GAP-088';
UPDATE "gaps" SET "description" = 'pages_read ON A SCAN ROW COUNTS THE PAGES HELD, NOT THE PAGES READ, SO A POINTER SCAN READS AS A FULL READ. TOOLING PR (rule 10). Found in sessions session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch and session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2 (PRs 211 and 212); the adversarial findings are in adversarial_findings, passes 8 and 9. record-scan computes pages_read against pages_total from the text file; it cannot record that 19 of 173 pages were read in full. The row for REF-00468 shows 173 of 173 beside a note saying about twenty. Scan notes cannot be amended, so each correction is a new scan row for the same source and mode (several now exist per source). RESOLVE: a pages-read-in-full field set by the operator and checked against the page markers of the quotes the rows use; and an amend-scan verb that appends a dated correction as amend-search does. DERIVE: select ref_id, pages_read, pages_total, method, substr(notes, 1, 80) from source_scans where created_by_session like ''session_2026-11-06%''. || CORRECTED 2026-11-06: RESOLUTION ANALYSIS (Opus, read-only, 2026-11-06; scratchpad/session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2/resolution-analysis-GAP-074-096.md, section for this gap). PARTLY. pages_read = pages held is documented design; the defect is the ledger (new ledger gap). Closed by that fix.', "updated_at" = '2026-11-06 05:29' WHERE "gap_id" = 'GAP-089';
UPDATE "gaps" SET "description" = 'THE VERBATIM CHECK CANNOT QUOTE A KOREAN SENTENCE THAT CROSSES A LINE BREAK, SO claim_text IS CUT MID-WORD. TOOLING PR (rule 10). Found in sessions session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch and session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2 (PRs 211 and 212); the adversarial findings are in adversarial_findings, passes 8 and 9. A PDF-extracted Korean line break can fall inside a word or between a stem and its ending; whitespace normalisation turns it into a space the source does not have. Extraction 215 quotes a sentence that stops at the break (...1.2미터 이상으) and extraction 209 starts at one (에는 1.5미터...), so their claim_text is a fragment and, for 209, drops its own condition (carried by the edge quote instead). Same class as GAP-056 (CJK numerals). RESOLVE: for CJK text compare with all whitespace removed, then store the quote as written. DERIVE: select extraction_id, claim_text from source_value_extractions where ref_id = ''REF-01031'' and extraction_id in (209, 215). || CORRECTED 2026-11-06: RESOLUTION ANALYSIS (Opus, read-only, 2026-11-06; scratchpad/session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2/resolution-analysis-GAP-074-096.md, section for this gap). WRONG (verified by the author). The verbatim check strips all whitespace: quote_in_artefacts returns True for both full Korean sentences (the 1.2 / 1.5 m corridor sentence and the access-route sentence). The quotes in extractions 209 and 215 were cut short by hand, not by the tool. The appended note on GAP-056 rests on the same false claim. Close as accepted; record the full quotes in notes.', "updated_at" = '2026-11-06 05:29' WHERE "gap_id" = 'GAP-090';
UPDATE "gaps" SET "description" = 'A SEARCH RUN BECAUSE A CRITIC FOUND A QUERY-SHAPE FAILURE CANNOT BE LABELLED adversarial-pass AT WRITE TIME. TOOLING PR (rule 10). Found in sessions session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch and session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2 (PRs 211 and 212); the adversarial findings are in adversarial_findings, passes 8 and 9. log-search --origin adversarial-pass requires --origin-pass-id, and the pass row exists only after record-adversarial-pass, which needs the critic''s transcript committed, which comes after the searches. --origin incidental refuses any target flag. Executions 199 and 200 were therefore logged incidental without targets, which removes them from the coverage counts they belong to. RESOLVE: accept a deferred pass id (backfilled by amend-search --set-origin-pass-id, which already exists NULL to value) and allow targets with origin adversarial-pass. DERIVE: select exec_id, origin, target_tier from search_executions where exec_id in (199, 200). || CORRECTED 2026-11-06: RESOLUTION ANALYSIS (Opus, read-only, 2026-11-06; scratchpad/session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2/resolution-analysis-GAP-074-096.md, section for this gap). PARTLY. A sanctioned two-step path exists (amend-search --set-origin adversarial-pass, then --set-origin-pass-id); the proposed targets with adversarial-pass contradicts the owner''s OQ-7 answer. Close as accepted; relabel executions 199 and 200 by the existing verb.', "updated_at" = '2026-11-06 05:29' WHERE "gap_id" = 'GAP-091';
UPDATE "gaps" SET "description" = 'A CATALOGUE READING DEPENDS ON THE READER''S KEYWORDS, AND THEY MISSED STATED FIGURES IN BOTH BATCHES. PROCESS AND TOOLING. Found in sessions session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch and session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2 (PRs 211 and 212); the adversarial findings are in adversarial_findings, passes 8 and 9. Twice running, the pre-screen of a persisted text keyed on the words the reader expected (anchura libre, headroom, 2000 mm, 1500 mm x 1500) and missed the same quantity in another spelling (ancho de paso libre, 2.1m, 1500mm x 1500mm, 22 N on a page the note said was not examined). Every absence note was then false and had to be found by a second reader. The sources are text; the number-and-unit statements in them can be inventoried mechanically per page. RESOLVE: a derived figure inventory (every number with a unit or a ratio, with its page and surrounding sentence) printed beside the parameter list for a catalogue reading, so an absence claim is checked against the inventory instead of a keyword; and an absence statement in a scan note must cite the sweep. This replaces nothing and adds one reader (rule 8 and the net-negative rule apply: name what it reads). DERIVE: compare the first and second scan notes for REF-00468, REF-01032 and REF-01028 in source_scans. || CORRECTED 2026-11-06: The net-negative rule named in the RESOLVE paragraph no longer applies: the owner''s 2026-11-06 ruling (references/project-standards.md, ''Owner rulings 2026-11-06'') drops the net-negative requirement and asks for the bare essentials in the most code-efficient form. What stays is naming what the added code reads and what wrong thing reaches the guidebook without it. Size any figure inventory against that, and prefer a rule or an existing sweep to new code if either closes the defect. || CORRECTED 2026-11-06: RESOLUTION ANALYSIS (Opus, read-only, 2026-11-06; scratchpad/session_2026-11-06-pipeline-batch-2/resolution-analysis-GAP-074-096.md, section for